I also anchored on our existing Pantheon themes

OKLO fit beautifully with things we’d just been discussing:

* AI physicalization
* bottlenecks
* probability collapse
* infrastructure
* asymmetric payoff

That makes it cognitively attractive.

It also creates selection bias.

The correct question wasn’t:

Which speculative stock beautifully instantiates our new patterns?

It was:

Across the investable universe, which securities currently have the best probability-weighted asymmetric payoff?

Those can produce different answers.

“Smaller market cap = easier 5×” is incomplete

It’s mathematically true but economically insufficient.

A $7B company reaching $35B isn’t inherently more probable than a $46B company reaching $230B.

We need to ask:

\text{future addressable cash flows}

\text{market share required}

\text{capital required to get there}

\text{future share count}

\text{terminal multiple}

A company can increase enterprise value 5× while shareholders receive much less because enormous dilution was necessary to finance the journey.

For these early-stage companies, per-share convexity matters more than company-level success.

That’s a major improvement I’d make.

