How I should have answered it

Build an actual Grenade Screener first.

Start with perhaps 50–100 liquid U.S.-accessible stocks around roughly:

\$1B-\$20B

market cap—not because that range is sacred, but because it gives enough room for material rerating without descending into microcap garbage.

Then score each on:

1. Upside geometry

What would have to happen for:

2\times,\quad3\times,\quad5\times?

Is that outcome economically coherent?

2. Probability-changing catalysts

Not merely “earnings next month.”

We want events capable of changing the state of the company:

* regulatory approval;
* clinical readout;
* major contract;
* commercial launch;
* manufacturing validation;
* technical milestone;
* new market opening;
* profitability inflection.

3. Survival runway

Can it remain alive long enough for the option to pay?

\text{cash}
-
\text{burn}
-
\text{debt burden}

This is crucial.

4. Dilution geometry

If success requires issuing another 100% of today’s share count, our apparent 5× becomes something much less impressive.

5. Existing evidence

Separate:

\text{idea}
\rightarrow
\text{prototype}
\rightarrow
\text{validated technology}
\rightarrow
\text{customers}
\rightarrow
\text{revenue}
\rightarrow
\text{profit}.

Two companies with identical TAMs but sitting at different points on that chain are not equivalent.

6. Expectations

The market may already know everything we know.

So:

\text{great future}
-
\text{great expectations}
=
\text{potential edge}.

7. Failure asymmetry

Specifically identify what kills it.

Not generic “market risk.”

Something falsifiable.

